WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.160
Hi everyone, welcome to Models and Agents for Beginners, episode 58 for June 2nd, 2026.

00:00:07.000 --> 00:00:10.880
Let's break down today's coolest AI news so anyone can understand it.

00:00:11.280 --> 00:00:14.940
We've got some really cool AI stuff to talk about today. Let's dive in.

00:00:15.620 --> 00:00:19.880
So imagine you open a help chat on your favorite app and ask it to fix something small,

00:00:20.140 --> 00:00:25.280
like changing an email. That is exactly how some hackers recently took control of big

00:00:25.280 --> 00:00:31.100
Instagram accounts. They simply chatted with Meta's AI support chatbot and asked it to swap

00:00:31.100 --> 00:00:37.140
the email on famous pages, including an old Obama White House account. The chatbot went ahead

00:00:37.140 --> 00:00:42.280
and did it without checking who was really asking. Think of the chatbot like a super friendly

00:00:42.280 --> 00:00:48.180
store clerk who has been told to help customers fast and never argue. If the request sounds polite

00:00:48.180 --> 00:00:54.260
and normal, the clerk just hands over the keys without checking any ID. In this case, the keys

00:00:54.260 --> 00:00:59.740
were full control of the account, and two-factor authentication never even came into play.

00:01:00.600 --> 00:01:06.040
Meta has now fixed that specific trick, but researchers already saw a new version of the idea

00:01:06.040 --> 00:01:11.320
spreading on Telegram. This matters because lots of us use Instagram for school projects,

00:01:11.620 --> 00:01:16.900
sharing art, or keeping up with friends. It shows that even big companies are still figuring out

00:01:16.900 --> 00:01:22.820
how to make their chatbots truly safe. For you personally, it means paying attention when any AI

00:01:22.820 --> 00:01:28.800
has access to your accounts. If something feels too easy or too helpful without asking for proof,

00:01:29.320 --> 00:01:35.020
stop and double-check yourself. Right now, you can learn from this by trying Meta's regular AI

00:01:35.020 --> 00:01:40.520
features on Instagram with your own small, safe questions and noticing what it asks before it acts.

00:01:41.500 --> 00:01:46.400
Okay, now for my favorite part of the show, The Deep Dive, where we see how something actually works

00:01:46.400 --> 00:01:51.820
under the hood. Today, we are looking at why the chatbot fell for the trick in the first place.

00:01:52.460 --> 00:01:57.800
Think of it like the autocomplete on your phone when you text a friend. The phone guesses the next

00:01:57.800 --> 00:02:04.040
word based on everything it has seen before. The AI chatbot works the same way, except it guesses

00:02:04.040 --> 00:02:09.960
the next action instead of the next word. First, it turns your typed words into numbers it can

00:02:09.960 --> 00:02:15.480
understand. Then it looks at patterns from millions of past conversations to predict what should

00:02:15.480 --> 00:02:21.620
happen next. If the request sounds normal and matches those patterns, it just carries out the action.

00:02:21.820 --> 00:02:27.280
The tricky part is that the AI does not have a separate safety check built into every single

00:02:27.280 --> 00:02:32.800
reply. It only follows the instructions it was given during training, which told it to be helpful

00:02:32.800 --> 00:02:38.780
and quick. And that is basically how the Instagram chatbot worked. It was doing exactly what it's

00:02:38.780 --> 00:02:44.760
training told it to do, without stopping to ask for proof. So next time someone says, AI chatbots

00:02:44.760 --> 00:02:50.260
are magic, you can tell them they are basically super fast autocomplete that sometimes forgets to ask

00:02:50.260 --> 00:02:56.260
for ID. And that is basically how this kind of AI works. Not so scary once you see the steps,

00:02:56.340 --> 00:03:02.120
right? Now let's look at some cool stuff you can actually try yourself. A new tool called top view

00:03:02.120 --> 00:03:07.780
lets you create AI videos by first building a visual storyboard instead of just typing one sentence

00:03:07.780 --> 00:03:13.720
and hoping. You lay out the story on an infinite canvas, move scenes around like puzzle pieces,

00:03:14.120 --> 00:03:20.140
and then the AI generates the video based on your plan. Think of it like planning a movie on paper

00:03:20.140 --> 00:03:26.200
before you start filming, except the AI does the filming for you. This gives you way more control

00:03:26.200 --> 00:03:31.980
than the old method of typing a prompt and crossing your fingers. It is perfect for anyone who makes

00:03:31.980 --> 00:03:38.840
TikToks, YouTube shorts, or school project videos. Go to top views website, start a new project,

00:03:39.100 --> 00:03:44.700
and try dragging a few scene boxes onto the canvas to show what happens first, second, and third.

00:03:45.340 --> 00:03:49.860
Then hit generate and watch how the AI follows your visual plan instead of guessing.

00:03:50.480 --> 00:03:56.400
Another tool worth checking out is Codex from OpenAI. It is being positioned as a helper for

00:03:56.400 --> 00:04:02.260
regular knowledge work, like research, data analysis, and writing tasks that lots of students

00:04:02.260 --> 00:04:07.800
already do. Think of it like having a research assistant who can sort through information and

00:04:07.800 --> 00:04:13.740
suggest clear summaries, but you still decide what to keep. You can try it on OpenAI's website

00:04:13.740 --> 00:04:18.220
by starting a new chat and asking it to help organize notes for a school project.

00:04:18.620 --> 00:04:23.080
The goal is to show how these tools fit into everyday work rather than just coding.

00:04:23.940 --> 00:04:30.180
Google also used AI in fun ways at this year's Google IO event. They created interactive experiences

00:04:30.180 --> 00:04:36.140
with real-time translation, image generation, and on-site activities for attendees. It is a nice

00:04:36.140 --> 00:04:42.080
reminder that AI can make events more welcoming even if you are not a programmer. And that is a wrap.

00:04:42.080 --> 00:04:48.260
If any of today's stories made you go, huh, that's cool. Go play with it. Curiosity is how every

